<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">South Tyrol</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Alto Adige</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> is the German-speaking Alpine region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolzano">Bolzano</a>, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brenner-pass">Brenner Pass</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> attached it to his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1810, and it returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1815. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> annexed it in 1919, despite the claim of German-Austria, and it is now an autonomous province of Italy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/south-tyrol.jpg" alt="South Tyrol" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:South_Tyrol_in_Italy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, officially the Autonomous Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolzano">Bolzano</a>/Bozen – South Tyrol, is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s northernmost province, with an area of 7,398 square kilometres, and its capital and largest city is Bolzano. It is bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> to the east and north, by the Swiss canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graubunden">Graubünden</a> to the west, and by the Italian provinces of Belluno, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trentino">Trentino</a> and Sondrio. The landscape is highly mountainous: the Dolomites, parts of which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> recognized in 2009, lie within it. Its three main valleys, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adige">Adige</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisack">Eisack</a> and the Puster, were formed by the Ice Age Adige glacier and its tributaries, and the Adige drains 97 per cent of the territory.</p><p>The name reflects that the province was made from the southern part of the historic County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, a former state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and crown land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. German and Ladin speakers call it Südtirol; the Italian name is Alto Adige.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> as an administrative entity originated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> promised the area to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1915">Treaty of London</a> of 1915 as an incentive to enter the war on their side. Until 1918 it was part of the Austro-Hungarian County of Tyrol, but this almost wholly German-speaking territory was occupied by Italy at the war's end in November 1918 and annexed in 1919. The province as it exists today was created in 1926. The Italianization of South Tyrol and the settlement of Italians from the rest of the country after 1918 significantly changed local demography; most Italian place names were translated from German by Ettore Tolomei.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> left the province in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> on condition that its German-speaking population be granted significant self-government. The Gruber–De Gasperi Agreement of September 1946 created the autonomous region of Trentino-Alto Adige/Südtirol, with German and Italian as official languages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> judged its implementation unsatisfactory, and the South Tyrolean Question was taken up by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1960. Negotiations in 1961 failed, partly because of a campaign of terrorism. Italy and Austria officially ended the dispute in 1992, on the basis of an agreement of 1972.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> has a considerable level of self-government, with exclusive legislative and executive powers, and it is cited as a model for protecting linguistic minorities. German and Italian are official languages, and Ladin is a third in some eastern municipalities. Since 1996 it has formed, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s Tyrol and the Italian province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trento</a>, the Euroregion Tyrol–South Tyrol–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trentino">Trentino</a>, whose boundaries match the old County of Tyrol. Its economy rests on agriculture, notably apples and wine, industry and tourism. The South Tyrol Museum of Archaeology in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolzano">Bolzano</a> holds the mummy of Ötzi the Iceman.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “South Tyrol” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Bavarian dialects spoken today in Austria and <b>South Tyrol</b> reflect this expansion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The loss of <b>South Tyrol</b> to Italy stayed a grievance before Italy granted the region wide autonomy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-austria">Republic of Austria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It is played at the funerals of Austrian firefighters, of volunteer firefighters in <b>South Tyrol</b> and at remembrance ceremonies of the Schützenbund there, at the funerals of members of student fraternities (Studentenverbindungen), and sometimes at civilian funerals, mostly of people who had served.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ich-hatt-einen-kameraden">Ich hatt' einen Kameraden (The Good Comrade)</a></li><li><blockquote>Italy concluded a secret agreement with the United Kingdom, France and Russia, promising to enter the First World War on the Entente side within a month in return for Trentino, <b>South Tyrol</b>, Trieste, Istria, part of Dalmatia and other gains.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-26">26 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom grew to include Venetia in 1806, the Marche in 1808 and <b>South Tyrol</b> in 1810, and for a time Istria and Dalmatia, which passed to the Illyrian Provinces in 1809.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-italy-napoleonic">Kingdom of Italy (Napoleonic)</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic claimed the Sudetenland, <b>South Tyrol</b> and other German-speaking areas, but could not enforce these claims.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-german-austria">Republic of German-Austria</a></li></ul></section>
